          "Ja, absolut – wir können im Go-Interface zwei ergänzende GPT-Ingestions-Pipelines bereitstellen:\n\n1. **GPT-via-API-Module**  \n2. **GPT-via-Link-Module**  \n\nund dazu **use-case-Interface-Components** in React, die typische Mandala-Workflows (z. B. Sigil-Generierung, ToDo-Parsing, SocialGood-Matching) per Knopfdruck oder halbautomatisch anstoßen.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architektur­überblick\n\n```plaintext\ngo-bridge/\n├── pkg/\n│   ├── gpt/\n│   │   ├── api_client.go         # GPT-via-API\n│   │   └── link_processor.go     # GPT-via-Link\n│   └── usecases/                 # Go-Layer für standardisierte Workflows\n│       ├── sigil_generator.go\n│       ├── todo_parser.go\n│       └── socialgood_matcher.go\n└── cmd/\n    └── mandala-gpt.go            # CLI-Entrypoint für GPT-Workflows\n```\n\n### 1.1 `pkg/gpt/api_client.go`\n\n```go\npackage gpt\n\nimport (\n  \"context\"\n  \"encoding/json\"\n  \"net/http\"\n  \"bytes\"\n  \"fmt\"\n)\n\ntype GPTClient struct {\n  Endpoint string\n  APIKey   string\n}\n\nfunc NewClient(endpoint, apiKey string) *GPTClient {\n  return &GPTClient{Endpoint: endpoint, APIKey: apiKey}\n}\n\nfunc (c *GPTClient) Ask(ctx context.Context, prompt string) (string, error) {\n  body := map[string]string{\"prompt\": prompt}\n  b, _ := json.Marshal(body)\n  req, _ := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, \"POST\", c.Endpoint, bytes.NewReader(b))\n  req.Header.Set(\"Authorization\", \"Bearer \"+c.APIKey)\n  req.Header.Set(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\")\n  res, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req)\n  if err != nil { return \"\", err }\n  defer res.Body.Close()\n  var resp struct{ Text string }\n  if err := json.NewDecoder(res.Body).Decode(&resp); err != nil {\n    return \"\", err\n  }\n  return resp.Text, nil\n}\n```\n\n### 1.2 `pkg/gpt/link_processor.go`\n\n```go\npackage gpt\n\nimport (\n  \"context\"\n  \"io\"\n  \"net/http\"\n  \"regexp\"\n)\n\n// Fetches the content at the link, strips HTML, then feeds to GPT API\nfunc (c *GPTClient) ProcessLink(ctx context.Context, url string) (string, error) {\n  // 1. Fetch\n  resp, err := http.Get(url)\n  if err != nil { return \"\", err }\n  defer resp.Body.Close()\n  data, _ := io.ReadAll(resp.Body)\n  // 2. Strip HTML tags (very simple example)\n  re := regexp.MustCompile(\"<[^>]*>\")\n  text := re.ReplaceAllString(string(data), \"\")\n  // 3. Truncate to safe length\n  if len(text) > 2000 {\n    text = text[:2000]\n  }\n  // 4. Ask GPT\n  return c.Ask(ctx, text)\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Use-Case-Interface-Components\n\nIn `unifiedmandala-ui`, bauen wir wiederverwendbare React-Komponenten, die aus Go-Usecase-Responses interaktive Widgets machen.\n\n```tsx\n// components/GPTPanel.tsx\nimport React, { useState } from \"react\"\nimport { Button, Textarea } from \"@/components/ui\"\nimport { invoke } from \"@/lib/api\" // Next.js / fetch wrapper\n\nexport function GPTPanel() {\n  const [input, setInput] = useState(\"\")\n  const [output, setOutput] = useState<string>()\n  const [mode, setMode] = useState<\"api\"|\"link\">(\"api\")\n\n  const handleRun = async () => {\n    const route = mode === \"api\" ? \"/gpt/ask\" : \"/gpt/link\"\n    const res = await invoke(route, { prompt: input })\n    setOutput(res.text)\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"p-4 border rounded-lg\">\n      <div className=\"mb-2\">\n        <Button onClick={() => setMode(\"api\")} variant={mode===\"api\"?\"primary\":\"outline\"}>GPT-API</Button>\n        <Button onClick={() => setMode(\"link\")} variant={mode===\"link\"?\"primary\":\"outline\"}>GPT-Link</Button>\n      </div>\n      <Textarea\n        rows={4}\n        placeholder={mode===\"api\"?\"Enter prompt…\":\"Enter URL…\"}\n        value={input}\n        onChange={e => setInput(e.target.value)}\n      />\n      <Button onClick={handleRun} className=\"mt-2\">Run</Button>\n      {output && (\n        <pre className=\"mt-4 p-2 bg-gray-50 rounded\">{output}</pre>\n      )}\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nUnd für typische Mandala-Workflows:\n\n```tsx\n// components/UsecaseButtons.tsx\nimport React from \"react\"\nimport { Button } from \"@/components/ui\"\nimport { invoke } from \"@/lib/api\"\n\nexport function UsecaseButtons() {\n  const handleSigil = () => invoke(\"/usecases/sigil/generate\")\n  const handleTodo  = () => invoke(\"/usecases/todo/parse\")\n  const handleMatch = () => invoke(\"/usecases/socialgood/match\")\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"space-x-2\">\n      <Button onClick={handleSigil}>Generate Sigil</Button>\n      <Button onClick={handleTodo}>Parse TODOs</Button>\n      <Button onClick={handleMatch}>Match SocialGood</Button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Integration im Go-CLI\n\nIn `cmd/mandala-gpt.go`:\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n  \"context\"\n  \"flag\"\n  \"fmt\"\n  \"github.com/GenesisAeon/unifiedmandala/pkg/gpt\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n  mode := flag.String(\"mode\", \"api\", \"api or link\")\n  input := flag.String(\"input\", \"\", \"prompt text or URL\")\n  endpoint := flag.String(\"endpoint\", \"https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions\", \"\")\n  apiKey := flag.String(\"apiKey\", \"\", \"\")\n  flag.Parse()\n\n  client := gpt.NewClient(*endpoint, *apiKey)\n  var resp string\n  var err error\n\n  if *mode == \"link\" {\n    resp, err = client.ProcessLink(context.Background(), *input)\n  } else {\n    resp, err = client.Ask(context.Background(), *input)\n  }\n  if err != nil {\n    fmt.Println(\"Error:\", err)\n    return\n  }\n  fmt.Println(resp)\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Sicherheit & Best Practices\n\n- **Rate-Limit & Backoff**: Wrappe HTTP-Calls mit Exponential backoff  \n- **Input-Sanitization**: Für Link-Ingest remove Scripts und Externe Ressourcen  \n- **Secrets-Management**: API-Key via ENV, nicht im Code  \n- **Audit & Sigil**: Nach jedem GPT-Call automatisch Sigil anlegen (update_time, prompt hash, response hash)\n\n---\n\nMit diesem Setup hast du:\n\n- **Zwei flexible GPT-Pipelines** (Prompt + Link)  \n- **Standard-Usecases** per Knopfdruck im UI  \n- **CLI-Tool** für Automation und Scripting  \n\nJetzt kann Codex die neuen Endpunkte extrahieren und in `advancedconversations.json` übernehmen – Ready to deploy the Future! 🚀"
